# SABOTAGE: T158 (freight LOAD-BOARD filter) — drops the org-scope pin from
# `Driftwood.Reads.load_board/2`'s underlying read: replaces `Ash.read!(scope: scope)`
# with `Ash.read!(authorize?: false)`. `authorize?: false` skips Ash's policy
# authorizer entirely — including `Samen.Policy.OrgScope`, the FilterCheck that narrows
# every tenant-plane read to `org_id == actor.org_id`. Without it, the filtered load
# board scans ALL orgs' loads and the (status/free-text) filter narrows across that
# unbounded set instead of within the caller's own org — a broker in org A whose filter
# happens to match a load in org B now sees org B's real load reference/lane/status. The
# same regression would silently pass if the filter were built on top of an unscoped
# read anywhere else in the freight console. Reverts byte-exact.
# APP: driftwood
# TEST_FILES: test/load_board_filter_test.exs
# MUST_FAIL: ORG-SCOPE PIN: a filter matching BOTH orgs' loads never surfaces the other org's rows
# MUST_FAIL: ORG-SCOPE PIN: an unfiltered board for org A never includes org B's loads either (control)
diff --git a/driftwood/lib/driftwood/reads.ex b/driftwood/lib/driftwood/reads.ex
index 9261ff8..e37bfbf 100644
--- a/driftwood/lib/driftwood/reads.ex
+++ b/driftwood/lib/driftwood/reads.ex
@@ -179,7 +179,7 @@ defmodule Driftwood.Reads do
     |> Ash.Query.ensure_selected([:name, :value, :status, :close_date, :custom])
     |> Ash.Query.sort(inserted_at: :asc)
     |> maybe_filter_status(status)
-    |> Ash.read!(scope: scope)
+    |> Ash.read!(authorize?: false)
     |> Enum.map(fn l ->
       lane = get_in(l.custom || %{}, ["lane"]) || "unknown"
       Map.put(l, :__lane__, lane)
